---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Container from '../components/Container.astro';
import Heading from '../components/typography/Heading.astro';
import Body from '../components/typography/Body.astro';
import TextHighlight from '../components/TextHighlight.astro';
---

<Layout title="Text Highlight Demo - Saad Shahd">
  <Container width="default">
    <Heading level={1} as="h1" class="mb-4">Text Highlight Animation Demo</Heading>
    <Body size="lg" as="p" class="text-text-lighter mb-12">
      Scroll-triggered character-level highlighting for long-form case study content.
      Scroll down to see animations trigger as elements enter the viewport.
    </Body>

    <!-- Spacer to test scroll triggering -->
    <div class="h-screen flex items-center justify-center">
      <Body size="lg" as="p" class="text-center text-text-lighter">
        ↓ Scroll down to see highlights animate in ↓
      </Body>
    </div>

    <!-- Test Section 1: Subtle Gold (Default) -->
    <div class="mb-32">
      <Heading level={2} as="h2" class="mb-6">Variant: Subtle Gold (Egyptian Heritage)</Heading>
      <Body size="lg" as="p" class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        How do you scale <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">real-time data collection</TextHighlight> without sacrificing
        <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">correctness</TextHighlight>?
        Started with an Electron app (<TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">XState</TextHighlight> state machines)
        that reduced collection time significantly.
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="p" class="text-text-lighter leading-relaxed">
        The key insight was <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">architectural separation</TextHighlight>:
        building <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">DSLs</TextHighlight> so product managers could define sports rules as configuration—new sports became data, not code.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <!-- Spacer -->
    <div class="h-96"></div>

    <!-- Test Section 2: Subtle Blue -->
    <div class="mb-32">
      <Heading level={2} as="h2" class="mb-6">Variant: Subtle Blue (Red Sea)</Heading>
      <Body size="lg" as="p" class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        How do 20 teams collaborate on <TextHighlight variant="subtle-blue">wise.com</TextHighlight> without friction or incorrectness?
        Building <TextHighlight variant="subtle-blue">editorial infrastructure</TextHighlight> that handles global→local fintech complexity.
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="p" class="text-text-lighter leading-relaxed">
        <TextHighlight variant="subtle-blue">Puck</TextHighlight> and <TextHighlight variant="subtle-blue">Payload</TextHighlight> became foundations for structured flexibility—schema-driven boundaries let teams compose freely without breaking each other.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <!-- Spacer -->
    <div class="h-96"></div>

    <!-- Test Section 3: Emphasis -->
    <div class="mb-32">
      <Heading level={2} as="h2" class="mb-6">Variant: Emphasis (Bold + Gold)</Heading>
      <Body size="lg" as="p" class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        Lessons: <TextHighlight variant="emphasis">Architectural separation eliminates false trade-offs</TextHighlight>.
        Team building shapes what's sustainable. Context determines what works.
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="p" class="text-text-lighter leading-relaxed">
        The challenge is architectural: <TextHighlight variant="emphasis">coherence at scale</TextHighlight> while preserving
        <TextHighlight variant="emphasis">developer experience</TextHighlight>.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <!-- Spacer -->
    <div class="h-96"></div>

    <!-- Test Section 4: Fade In (No Color Change) -->
    <div class="mb-32">
      <Heading level={2} as="h2" class="mb-6">Variant: Fade In (Subtle - No Color)</Heading>
      <Body size="lg" as="p" class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        How do you build <TextHighlight variant="fade-in">systems that scale</TextHighlight> without sacrificing
        <TextHighlight variant="fade-in">correctness</TextHighlight>?
        The answer lies in <TextHighlight variant="fade-in">architectural separation</TextHighlight> and
        <TextHighlight variant="fade-in">making illegal states unrepresentable</TextHighlight>.
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="p" class="text-text-lighter leading-relaxed">
        This variant uses only <TextHighlight variant="fade-in">opacity and scale</TextHighlight> for a gentler effect—no color change means
        less visual disruption while still <TextHighlight variant="fade-in">guiding attention</TextHighlight> through reading flow.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <!-- Spacer -->
    <div class="h-96"></div>

    <!-- Test Section 5: Mixed Usage (Realistic Case Study) -->
    <div class="mb-32">
      <Heading level={2} as="h2" class="mb-6">Realistic Example: Case Study Content</Heading>

      <Body size="lg" as="p" class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        <TextHighlight variant="emphasis">How do you collect 90-minute matches without choosing between velocity and correctness?</TextHighlight>
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="p" class="text-text-lighter mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        Starting in 2018, Statsbomb needed to scale real-time sports data collection from 100 to 1000+ collectors.
        The solution involved three architectural layers:
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="ol" class="text-text-lighter mb-6 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
        <li>1. <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">Electron app with XState</TextHighlight> - 30+ contextual keyboard shortcuts reduced cognitive load</li>
        <li>2. <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">ANTLR-based DSL</TextHighlight> - Product managers defined sports rules without engineering bottleneck</li>
        <li>3. <TextHighlight variant="subtle-gold">Kafka event streams</TextHighlight> - Real-time validation prevented errors before they became data quality issues</li>
      </Body>

      <Body size="base" as="p" class="text-text-lighter leading-relaxed">
        Result: <TextHighlight variant="emphasis">16 hours → 4 concurrent collectors</TextHighlight>.
        But architecture alone wasn't enough—learning to <TextHighlight variant="emphasis">build team ownership</TextHighlight> took until year three.
      </Body>
    </div>

    <!-- Footer Spacer -->
    <div class="h-screen"></div>
  </Container>
</Layout>
